Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.932
KEITH DANEKIND: Welcome to
the "Apigee Up Close" webcast.

00:00:05.932 --> 00:00:07.640
We do these twice a
month, and the topics

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.470
change based on demand.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:10.480
Hi, I'm Keith Danekind.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.043
I'm an Apigee customer engineer.

00:00:12.043 --> 00:00:13.460
So for today's
agenda, we're going

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:17.680
to do a brief overview of API
management and Apigee Edge.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.430
Then I'm going to do
a demo on Apigee Edge

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:20.888
and show you a few
things in there.

00:00:20.888 --> 00:00:22.890
And then I'll take
some questions.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.300
Let's get started.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.600
So let's talk about
API management.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.190
Let's consider a scenario.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:30.410
Let's suppose you've got
some services over there

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.315
on the right-hand
side, so anything

00:00:32.315 --> 00:00:34.190
you happen to have around
REST services, SOAP

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.950
services, microservices,
maybe it's

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.350
your Service Bus,
anything over there.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.600
We've got some folks over
here on the left-- partners,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.180
employees, customers-- and
they have applications.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.930
And those applications need to
get data from these services.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.840
There's a need to control what
those applications can do,

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.090
and how they can access that
data and how frequently,

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:54.978
and things like that.

00:00:54.978 --> 00:00:56.520
That's where API
management comes in.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.820
That's where Apigee
Edge fits in here.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.860
So we put Apigee Edge
in here to control

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.017
how those applications can
access that right-hand side.

00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:06.350
Now, we also have to think
about application developers,

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.520
the people who write
all those applications.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.030
How will they gain
access to these systems?

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.400
What do we need to do in
order to get them credentials

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.045
so that Apigee will let them
into those backend services?

00:01:16.045 --> 00:01:17.420
How will they read
documentation?

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.045
How will they learn
how your APIs work?

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:21.230
And how will they
sign up for access?

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:23.690
Along the way, as traffic
starts to flow through here,

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:25.898
we'd also like to be able
to create some reports that

00:01:25.898 --> 00:01:27.920
tells us about which
applications are accessing

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.230
this, what they're doing, how
frequently they're calling,

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.855
what's the level of traffic, how
does it compare to past trends

00:01:32.855 --> 00:01:34.680
and things like that.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.260
So a few more things
we want to do.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.540
We could use Apigee in
here to build a facade

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.980
over the backend services.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:41.960
We can present one
set of interfaces

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.770
to those external applications
compared to what's inside.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:48.260
We can control access so
different applications might

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.880
have access to
different services,

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.220
and they might get different
things when they call them.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.613
We can rate limit to protect
those backend services.

00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:56.780
If one of those applications
is calling too quickly,

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:57.980
we can shut it down.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.840
We can also impose some quotas.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.757
Maybe we want to give
developers limited access

00:02:02.757 --> 00:02:05.090
to some of these things, and
we may want to charge money

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.890
for other things.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.840
We can add caching in there
to improve performance.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.170
So if we already have the
answer in Apigee Edge,

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.180
we don't need to
call the backend.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.430
We could reformat that
request as it comes in

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:18.967
and make it appropriate
for those backend services.

00:02:18.967 --> 00:02:21.050
The response that comes
from the backend services,

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.092
we could change that around
too before we send it

00:02:23.092 --> 00:02:24.552
back to the applications.

00:02:24.552 --> 00:02:26.510
We could manage a whole
collection of those app

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:29.030
developers out there and
manage their credentials.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.430
And along the way, we could
collect usage analytics.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.305
And that would give us
some insights into how

00:02:33.305 --> 00:02:34.795
our API program is going.

00:02:34.795 --> 00:02:36.170
So these are all
things you might

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.060
want to do with an API
management platform.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.990
And this is a good overview
of what API management is.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.570
There's a new way to think
about the problem of services.

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:46.070
Traditionally, we
would have thought

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:48.180
of this as an exposure problem.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.908
So we've got some data, we need
to expose it through a service.

00:02:51.908 --> 00:02:54.200
But there's a better way to
think about this, a pattern

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.690
that we've seen our most
successful customers adopt.

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.670
And that's to think
about it from the outside

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:00.820
in, meaning that rather
than just thinking

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.320
about exposing a service, what
we're actually thinking about

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.810
is the end user who will
use the application.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.850
What's that user's experience?

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:10.472
What is it like to use the
application that they're using?

00:03:10.472 --> 00:03:11.930
And how do we
empower the developer

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.220
who writes that application?

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.350
That comes from
having great APIs

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.210
and building a set
of APIs that are very

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:18.860
nice to use for that developer.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.080
And that's what
the API team does.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.990
So if we think about this
from the outside inward,

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:26.460
we'll have much better success.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.460
There are a lot of people
who use Apigee Edge.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.800
What's interesting for me
when I look at this slide is,

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.510
I'd look at all the
different businesses

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:33.350
that these people are in.

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.017
What they all have
in common is, they're

00:03:35.017 --> 00:03:37.610
seeing success from
having an API program,

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.200
either internal or external.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.450
So let's look at Apigee Edge.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.820
Apigee Edge is an API
management platform.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.770
And we can think of it
in three major parts.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.650
We can think of it as
a set of APIs services.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:51.190
That's the part
that routes traffic.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.070
That's the thing that we
put between the applications

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:53.890
and the services.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.515
And we can think of
some needs in there

00:03:55.515 --> 00:03:58.710
along the lines of security,
transforming messages, acting

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.840
as an enterprise
gateway, orchestrating

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.390
those backend services,
and preventing people

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:05.340
from abusing those services.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.972
In terms of
developer engagement,

00:04:06.972 --> 00:04:08.430
we need to have a
developer portal.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:11.040
And that's a good place to put
documentation for developers

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.870
to come who want to
consume these APIs

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.970
and read about how to use them.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:16.470
There's a need to
have API products,

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.332
so a way to take
all these services

00:04:18.332 --> 00:04:20.540
and bundle them together
into different packages that

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.570
provide different access.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:24.040
And there may be
a need to monetize

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.945
services, where we're going
to charge money for access.

00:04:26.945 --> 00:04:28.820
Finally, we need a way
to measure everything.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:31.150
And that's where API
Analytics comes in.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:32.650
We can measure
developer engagement,

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:34.360
we can measure
business metrics, we

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.970
can look at operational
metrics, API program metrics.

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:39.860
And we can monitor the platform
to see how it's working.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:43.440
So all of this together makes
an API management platform.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.160
So what's the difference between
an API Gateway and an API

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:47.660
management platform?

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:49.880
Well, an API Gateway
is a small subset

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:51.880
of an API management platform.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:54.620
As you can see from this slide,
an API management platform

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.970
does a lot more than you would
do with a simple gateway.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.580
So gateway would
do basic things,

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.620
like collect security tokens.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:03.050
But there's a lot more to
solving the API problem

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:05.712
than there is with
a simple gateway.

00:05:05.712 --> 00:05:07.170
So let's talk about
some advantages

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:11.310
that Apigee has over other
solutions in the marketplace.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.830
In Gartner's recent
Magic Quadrant survey,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.750
they found Apigee
to be a Leader.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.600
And they ranked Apigee highest
in terms of the ability

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.830
to execute and the
completeness of their vision.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.815
You can find more about
this on the Apigee website.

00:05:23.815 --> 00:05:25.190
There are a few
different options

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:27.410
in terms of how
you deploy Apigee.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:29.330
Apigee is offered as a
software as a service

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:30.830
managed in Google Cloud.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.680
You can run it on
premises, in a data center.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.670
You can run it in the
cloud of your choice.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:38.100
Or you can run hybrid, where
most of it runs in the cloud,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.600
and some of it's going to run
near your computing resources.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:43.600
These are all different options
for running Apigee Edge.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.660
Apigee Edge offers global
scale and availability.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.340
It's hosted in 13 data
centers around the world.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:53.650
And historically, we had
99.999% availability.

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:55.390
The Developer Portal
within Apigee Edge

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:57.693
can be easily customized
to meet your needs.

00:05:57.693 --> 00:05:59.110
If this is an
internal portal, you

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:00.070
might keep it kind of simple.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:01.653
But if it's external
facing, you might

00:06:01.653 --> 00:06:05.160
want to brand it to match
your branding of your website.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.230
Apigee gives you a choice
between configuration

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:08.045
or coding.

00:06:08.045 --> 00:06:10.170
There's a number of
out-of-the-box policies that do

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:10.720
basic things.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:12.470
That's what we're
seeing on the left here.

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:15.600
You can drop in these policies
to request an access token

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.390
or add response caching.

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:19.140
If you want to do
code-driven development,

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.570
you can build your own
policies, and you can drop those

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:23.760
in and mix them with the
out-of-the-box policies.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.310
And that can be done with
JavaScript, Java, or Python.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:28.950
And you can also
host Node.js in here.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.233
Apigee Edge has a
wonderful analytic system.

00:06:31.233 --> 00:06:33.150
You can use it to provide
real-time visibility

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.495
into the activity in the system.

00:06:35.495 --> 00:06:37.120
You can also use it
to measure and grow

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:38.865
your digital ecosystem.

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:40.490
So you can look at
operational metrics,

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:43.190
but you can also look
at business metrics.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:44.780
Apigee offers monetization.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:47.877
So if you decide you want to
charge for access to your APIs,

00:06:47.877 --> 00:06:49.460
there's a monetization
add-on that you

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:51.290
can get for Apigee to do this.

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:53.690
You can do revenue-sharing
models, fee-based models,

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:54.630
or freemium models.

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:56.360
Maybe the first
100 calls are free,

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:58.068
and if you want
something more than that,

00:06:58.068 --> 00:06:59.520
then you have to pay.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.390
Apigee Sense is another option.

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:03.780
You can add Apigee Sense
to do bot detection

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:05.730
and detect malicious traffic.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.950
So you won't necessarily
see this in the demo today,

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:11.010
but Apigee has a collection
of management APIs.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.950
And these let you control
every aspect of the platform

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.290
programmatically through
a REST-based API.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.570
This can be used to automate
operation of Apigee.

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:21.510
Maybe you want to automate
the creation of users

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.280
or credentials for people.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.290
Or maybe you want to
use this to push things

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.082
into production
automatically after running

00:07:27.082 --> 00:07:28.080
a series of tests.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.530
There's APIs to do all of that.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:32.780
There's a wonderful community
and a bunch of resources

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:33.830
to help you with Apigee.

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:37.880
So there's community.apigee.com
with over 10,000 users.

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:39.920
We've got a series of
four-minute videos, one

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:41.273
for each feature in the product.

00:07:41.273 --> 00:07:43.190
You can go in and learn
about the spike arrest

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:44.790
in four minutes, for example.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:46.790
There's a number of
webcasts that are out there.

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:48.068
You can find those on YouTube.

00:07:48.068 --> 00:07:50.360
There's customer stories,
where we interview customers,

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:52.235
and they talk about what
they do with Apigee.

00:07:52.235 --> 00:07:53.690
And we publish a
bunch of ebooks.

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.850
All of these are great
resources to help you become

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:57.870
productive with the platform.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.145
So let's look at a demo.

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:01.020
What we're going to look
at in the demo today

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:03.420
is a simple set of
APIs that sit on top

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:04.710
of a set of weather data.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:06.270
The weather data is in BigQuery.

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.920
And there's weather stations
from all around the world.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:10.920
You can look those up by
latitude and longitude.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.337
And for any given
weather station,

00:08:12.337 --> 00:08:14.560
you can pull down
historical weather.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:15.660
So this is what I've got.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.868
On the back, all the way on
the right-hand side here,

00:08:17.868 --> 00:08:20.010
we've got weather data
stored in BigQuery.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:21.570
In order to get
at that data, I've

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.000
got to log in using a service
account in Google Cloud.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.040
And in order to get
credentials for that,

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.540
I have to talk to Cloud
Identity and Access Management.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.600
So I'm going to send some
credentials over there.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:33.120
And it's going to return
to me an OAuth token.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:35.760
And with every request I make
into the weather database,

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:38.970
I've got to send that
credential as a header.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:40.799
On the left-hand side
of this, I didn't

00:08:40.799 --> 00:08:42.150
want to have that security.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:45.390
So what I'm doing instead
is, I'm using API keys.

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.130
So I have a single-page
web application,

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:48.660
I've got a few
mobile applications.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:51.150
Each of those has
their own API key.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.650
So I'm splitting the security
into two different parts here,

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:55.620
the backend security and
the frontend security.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:57.810
There's some other things
I'm going to do on here also.

00:08:57.810 --> 00:08:59.685
I want to build off a
facade on that backend,

00:08:59.685 --> 00:09:02.100
hide away the details
of where this data is.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.990
And I want to control
access to some features

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:05.850
so only limited things
you can do in here.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.770
I want to rate limit to
protect that backend.

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:09.510
I don't want people
calling us too much.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.010
And I'm going to
impose some quotas.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.012
So depending on which
API product you're using,

00:09:13.012 --> 00:09:14.220
you'll have different quotas.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:17.070
And I'm going to add
caching to help performance.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:19.140
I'm going to reformat the
request as it comes in

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:20.973
and the response when
it comes back in order

00:09:20.973 --> 00:09:22.920
to make this more
friendly to my developers

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:25.080
that want to use this API.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.820
In the demo, we're going to
consider three perspectives.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:30.600
The first one is the application
developer/API consumer.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.730
And that person might be
saying, "All I want to do

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:33.870
is build an application.

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.990
Make it easy for me to
consume your services."

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:38.400
We'll look at the API developer.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:40.140
They might be saying,
"I need to share

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:42.930
a service with other people,
but with some limits."

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.310
And we'll look at the API team.

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:45.685
And they might be
saying, "I want

00:09:45.685 --> 00:09:47.550
to think about APIs
as software products.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:50.010
There are customers,
lifecycles, versions.

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:52.200
I want to measure my
program and tune it."

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:55.112
As a developer, I might start
here on the Developer Portal.

00:09:55.112 --> 00:09:56.820
From here, I can get
what I need in order

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:59.310
to be productive with these
APIs and to start using them

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:01.043
in my application.

00:10:01.043 --> 00:10:03.210
I see there's a number of
API products and something

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:04.720
that says Get Started on here.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:06.940
Let's look at the
available API products.

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.340
So I can see that there
are a few APIs available.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:12.030
Let's look at this one.

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:15.200
So here's some documentation
on how these APIs work.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.630
I'm looking at the
Weather Stations API.

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:18.720
There's the URL in there.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:21.950
And it looks like I
can put in a location.

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:23.137
And I need an API key.

00:10:23.137 --> 00:10:25.220
And then it'll give me a
list of weather stations.

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:27.860
So I'd like to
actually try this out.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.165
To do that, I'm going to go up
and register a new application.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:42.643
We'll call it the
webcast demo app.

00:10:42.643 --> 00:10:44.310
And we're going to
go with this package.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.740
Now, that's registered
my application,

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:56.120
and I've got an API key.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.790
Let's go back to the
docs and give this a try.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.037
I'll put my API key in there.

00:11:06.037 --> 00:11:07.245
And we'll put it in location.

00:11:10.875 --> 00:11:12.000
I'm near Boulder, Colorado.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.940
Let's check the
weather for there.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:16.650
And I see a list
of weather stations

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:19.450
that are near Boulder, Colorado.

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:22.680
So what I like about this is, I
can try out the API right here.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:26.400
And I can get an idea of
what I need to send in,

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:28.400
and what will come
back in the response.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:29.900
And that makes me
pretty productive.

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:31.870
I could start coding
at this point.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:33.780
So I visited the
Developer Portal.

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:36.690
I read the documentation
on how to use the API.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:38.335
And then I registered
an application,

00:11:38.335 --> 00:11:39.710
and that returned
the key for me.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:41.418
I took the key over
to the documentation,

00:11:41.418 --> 00:11:43.140
and I was able to
test it out in there.

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:44.723
And I have a pretty
good feel for what

00:11:44.723 --> 00:11:46.440
it's like to call the API now.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.683
So I can make a call in
here and get data back.

00:11:48.683 --> 00:11:50.850
The next thing to do is,
let's write an application.

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:53.740
So I've got a simple application
that I've written here.

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:55.830
And what this
allows you to do is,

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:57.480
you can search for
weather stations

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:00.358
by typing in an address
or landmark up here.

00:12:00.358 --> 00:12:02.400
And then you get back a
list of weather stations.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.220
And you can check for high
and low temperature data

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:08.350
at that particular
weather station.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:09.600
So I'm near Boulder, Colorado.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:12.690
Let's look and see what weather
stations are around here.

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:18.080
So I type in this location.

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:19.830
And it's going to find
me weather stations

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.120
that are near that location.

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:22.720
They get populated down here.

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:24.300
So it looks like
it's found a few.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:27.810
I'm going to go with this
one, specify a year in here.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:28.987
And then I can run a query.

00:12:28.987 --> 00:12:31.320
And that's going to pull down
the historical temperature

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:32.010
data.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:35.640
This is for the
Boulder location, 2015.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:37.590
And we can see the
high temperature

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:40.500
data and the low
temperature data in here.

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:41.620
So not bad.

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:42.870
This was pretty easy to write.

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:43.912
It took me about an hour.

00:12:43.912 --> 00:12:46.140
Most of it was spent trying
to format these graphs

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:47.730
and to make them look nice.

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:49.110
I added this little
status button

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:50.943
down here so we could
see what was returned.

00:12:50.943 --> 00:12:52.530
I got a nice 200 there.

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:55.930
If I run this a few times,
let's see what happens.

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:57.900
So I called too quickly,
and I got shut down

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.365
with some rate limiting.

00:13:00.365 --> 00:13:01.740
So that's our
simple application.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:03.960
Let's switch roles
to the API developer

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:08.210
and see what we had to build
in order to make this possible.

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:10.840
So as the API developer, there's
a few things I'm going to do.

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:13.480
What I did when I was building
this particular demo was,

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:15.730
first thing I did
was fire up Postman.

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:17.735
And I called my backend
service a few times,

00:13:17.735 --> 00:13:20.110
and played around with it,
read documentation and figured

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:21.130
out what it could do.

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:22.540
And that influenced
what I wanted

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:24.910
to do in terms of building
an API proxy for it.

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:27.550
So once I decided
on that, and I was

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:31.600
happy with how the back service
works, I came into here.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.510
And then I built a specification
to describe my API.

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:38.610
Let's look at that.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:40.520
So here's my Weather
History API spec.

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:46.758
I've described how it works and
the three different services

00:13:46.758 --> 00:13:48.300
you might call
underneath there-- one

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:51.720
to look up weather stations,
one to look up temperature data,

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.160
and one to look up wind data.

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:54.660
And I've specified
all that in here.

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:02.510
I can see what the documentation
is going to look like.

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:04.500
I've specified what
the results look like.

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:06.500
So this should give my
developers a good idea

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:10.230
of exactly how this works.

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:12.320
So the next step is
to build an API proxy.

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:14.390
You might come to this
screen under API Proxies

00:14:14.390 --> 00:14:17.440
and hit this Plus
Proxy button up here.

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:19.200
So we have a few
choices in here.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:21.780
Reverse proxy, the first
one, is the most common.

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:23.070
And this is where I started.

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:25.350
You can use an OpenAPI
spec if you have one.

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:26.850
Or you can just
start with the URL,

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:28.475
and make a straight
through connection,

00:14:28.475 --> 00:14:30.540
and start to build up
your proxy from there.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:32.040
The few other options
in here-- if I

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:34.770
was working with a SOAP service,
I might choose this second one.

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.020
And that gives me an option
to build either a straight

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:39.270
through connection to
an existing SOAP service

00:14:39.270 --> 00:14:40.950
or to possibly
build something that

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:44.310
does SOAP-to-REST and
REST-to-SOAP conversion.

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:47.970
I'd supply the WSDL, and it
would build something for me.

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:51.660
I might use No Target if
I wanted to mock this up.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:53.435
So what it does in
that case is, Apigee

00:14:53.435 --> 00:14:55.560
is going to return the
response, and it's not going

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:57.330
to talk to a backend system.

00:14:57.330 --> 00:15:00.270
So I might mock up the
response inside of this proxy.

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:04.620
And I could use that to provide
sample data to my developers.

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:08.117
Hosted Target and Node.js allows
me to host Node.js in here.

00:15:08.117 --> 00:15:10.200
And I might do that if I
were going to orchestrate

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:11.710
several backend systems.

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:13.650
And I want the
backend that's called

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:15.630
by Apigee to be this
Node.js code that

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:18.000
then calls other services.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.820
And if someone sent me a proxy
bundle that they've developed,

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:24.130
I would use this last
option to import it.

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:27.930
So I entered my spec, and that
created my API proxy for me

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:29.090
and put me into the editor.

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:30.007
So let's look at that.

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:35.590
So let's look at
the existing API.

00:15:35.590 --> 00:15:38.950
This is where I'll develop the
API that outside consumers are

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:40.270
going to use.

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:43.480
Key things to notice here are
Target Endpoints down there.

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:46.660
And this is the backend URL
that's going to be called.

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:48.160
So instead of calling
that directly,

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:51.490
my consumers will now call
one of these URLs up here.

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:54.220
That talks to Apigee, and
then Apigee sends the request

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:55.900
to the backend.

00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:58.240
We also have, you'll notice,
there's two different URLs

00:15:58.240 --> 00:15:58.740
here.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:01.360
I have a few different
environments I can deploy into.

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.760
In my demo environment
here, I have

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:05.380
a test and a
production environment

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:06.670
that's available to me.

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:08.630
And I have version
7 in production

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:10.330
and version 8 in test.

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:13.720
So they'll call those URLs to
talk to the backend system.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:15.190
It'll be managed by Apigee.

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:17.720
And traffic will
flow back and forth.

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:22.420
So let's dig in a little further
and see what's over in Develop.

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:24.110
So we're over here
on the Develop tab.

00:16:24.110 --> 00:16:25.210
And what we'll
notice in here is,

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:27.168
there's a few different
sections to the screen.

00:16:27.168 --> 00:16:30.520
We go through those, and then
I'll go into some details.

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:32.560
So up here, we have
a graphical depiction

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:34.120
of the request and
the response flow.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:37.000
So the request comes in,
goes to the backend target,

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.692
and then the
response comes back.

00:16:38.692 --> 00:16:40.900
And along the way, we can
have some of these policies

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:43.192
in place, so they are going
to help manage this request

00:16:43.192 --> 00:16:44.860
and response flow.

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:47.600
Those policies are configured
down here at the bottom.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:48.850
So I'll click on one of these.

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:51.310
And you can see how it's
configured at the bottom.

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:54.310
Policies that I've used in
here are listed over here.

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:55.150
I can reuse these.

00:16:55.150 --> 00:16:56.640
I can drag and drop
them over here.

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:59.140
Things to the request go on the
top, things for the response

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:00.830
go on the bottom.

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:03.160
We have two sections down
here, Proxy Endpoints

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:04.690
and Target Endpoints.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:06.460
Proxy Endpoints
control what happens

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:07.990
between the client and Apigee.

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:09.700
And Target Endpoints
control what happens

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:12.550
between Apigee and the backend.

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:15.470
So for Proxy Endpoints, there's
a few different flows in here.

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:18.062
The PreFlow is executed
unconditionally

00:17:18.062 --> 00:17:18.770
at the beginning.

00:17:18.770 --> 00:17:21.665
So people might put different
things on the end of the URL.

00:17:21.665 --> 00:17:23.790
They might call this in
different ways with a POST,

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:25.869
or a PUT, or GET, or
something like that.

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:28.359
We may want to have the system
behave differently depending

00:17:28.359 --> 00:17:29.442
on how they're calling it.

00:17:29.442 --> 00:17:32.290
So PreFlow is always called
first, unconditionally.

00:17:32.290 --> 00:17:34.060
I tend to put things
in here like checking

00:17:34.060 --> 00:17:37.850
for security tokens and rate
limiting and things like that.

00:17:37.850 --> 00:17:40.120
The next few that I've got
in here are conditional.

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:43.930
So I can click in here,
and I can add a new one.

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:45.430
And I can choose a
path, and a verb,

00:17:45.430 --> 00:17:47.140
and some sort of condition.

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:48.940
So those are called
conditionally.

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:51.700
So if somebody calls my
URL with slash stations

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:55.090
at the end, that gets called if
they call that with a HTTP GET.

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:56.590
Same thing for
temperature and wind.

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:59.740
And then PostFlow is called
unconditionally at the end,

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:01.130
after the others execute.

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:04.750
So first PreFlow, anything
conditional, and then PostFlow.

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:07.630
The same conditions hold
true for the Target backends.

00:18:07.630 --> 00:18:09.415
In my case, I've
only got one backend.

00:18:09.415 --> 00:18:11.290
And I don't have any
specific logic in there.

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:14.270
But let's imagine we had a bunch
of different backend systems.

00:18:14.270 --> 00:18:16.733
And we might be doing some
dynamic routing among them.

00:18:16.733 --> 00:18:18.400
We can have different
conditions in here

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:22.160
for those different endpoints.

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:23.670
Down here, I've
got some Resources.

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:26.130
So these are JavaScript
files in this case.

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:28.010
So things like
code and files that

00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:30.650
are associated with this
API proxy go down here.

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:32.300
And I've got JavaScript
policies that

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:33.440
reference some source code.

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:36.510
And that's the source
code right there.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:39.780
So let's look a little bit
in more detail in here.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:41.670
So we have a few policies
in place up here.

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:42.770
Where did they come from?

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:45.980
I came over to here and I
hit the Plus Step button.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:48.770
And inside of here,
I'm greeted with a menu

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:51.522
of possible policies that
I could put in there.

00:18:51.522 --> 00:18:53.480
There's a few things
around traffic management.

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:57.140
There's some around security,
some around mediation,

00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:58.590
and then some extensions.

00:18:58.590 --> 00:19:00.200
So let's look at
each section here.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:02.700
Traffic Management is going
to do basic traffic management

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:03.200
things.

00:19:03.200 --> 00:19:05.765
So Quota-- quota would
limit how many times

00:19:05.765 --> 00:19:07.890
you can call something in
a certain period of time.

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:11.360
So maybe I want to limit
this to 100 calls per day.

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:13.640
Spike Arrest is going
to limit how it's

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:14.930
called but based on frequency.

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:17.070
So think of this like
a circuit breaker.

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:19.010
You don't want somebody
to call this too fast.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:21.420
I might set this to 100
transactions per second.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:23.480
And if they call at a
rate greater than that,

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:25.250
it'll return an error.

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:26.840
We have several
policies for caching,

00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:29.330
so basic Response
Cache and the ability

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:31.940
to manipulate these different
caches programmatically.

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:33.950
You can have named
caches, multiple caches.

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:36.020
They're going to have
different times to live.

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.312
And you can use them at
different points in your flows.

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:44.010
For security, we've got a
number of options in here.

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:46.993
Basic Auth is going to request
a username and password

00:19:46.993 --> 00:19:48.660
from somebody who is
calling the system.

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:50.285
Or it can be used to
construct a header

00:19:50.285 --> 00:19:53.460
to send to a backend
system that expects it.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:55.050
The next three
policies are there

00:19:55.050 --> 00:19:57.120
to make sure that bad
things don't get through

00:19:57.120 --> 00:19:59.360
in the request.

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:01.160
We have a full
implementation of OAuth 2.

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:04.580
We can check OAuth tokens,
we can generate OAuth tokens.

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:08.210
For backwards compatibility,
there's some OAuth 1.0 in here.

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:11.010
We have the ability
to verify API keys.

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:13.260
Access Control would
let us limit calls

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:14.940
to a certain range
of IP addresses.

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:18.360
We can blacklist or
whitelist certain IP ranges.

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:20.520
If we need to generate
or validate SAML,

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:21.940
we have SAML assertions in here.

00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:23.023
And we can work with JWTs.

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:28.010
Under mediation, we
have policies for JSON

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:29.730
to XML, XML to JSON.

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:31.290
We can raise custom
error messages,

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:32.610
check monetization limits.

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:35.760
We can do XSLT and SOAP
message validation.

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:38.100
Assign Message and Extract
Variables I use a lot.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:40.260
Assign Message lets
me change the request

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:41.920
or the response in some way.

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:44.820
So let's say I want
to insert a header,

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:46.800
or I want to take
something out of a URL

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:47.880
and put it into the body.

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:49.860
I can use that with
Assign Message.

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:51.870
Extract Variables lets
me grab something out

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:54.000
of that request or
response and use it

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:55.890
as a variable inside of Apigee.

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:58.380
Access Entity lets
us load up metadata.

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:01.410
And Key Value Operations lets
us work with the key value map,

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:02.970
so very nice key
value map inside

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:05.430
of Apigee Edge that lets
you store anything that you

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:06.690
need at different scopes.

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:09.030
So, for example, in
here, I store an API key

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:10.823
that I need to talk
to Google Maps.

00:21:10.823 --> 00:21:12.990
And I use the key value
map, pull that out of there,

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:17.050
stick it into a request,
and make my call.

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:18.580
So those are the
basic policies that

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:20.180
do things that are very common.

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:21.890
We also have some
extensions in here.

00:21:21.890 --> 00:21:25.510
So if you want to do things
in programming languages,

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:28.082
Java, Python, and
JavaScript are supported.

00:21:28.082 --> 00:21:30.040
With those languages,
you have complete control

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:32.170
over the request and response.

00:21:32.170 --> 00:21:34.463
There's variables in
there to be able to grab

00:21:34.463 --> 00:21:36.130
things like headers
and stuff like that.

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:37.150
Or the body content--

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:39.670
you can change it
around and replace it.

00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:41.830
We'll see an example
of that in my demo.

00:21:41.830 --> 00:21:44.320
Service Callout lets
me do an HTTP callout

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:45.860
before I call the backend.

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:48.400
So, for example, in
my demo I use that.

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:50.710
When I type in the
location of a city,

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:52.210
it makes a callout
to Google Maps

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:53.800
and returns a latitude
and longitude,

00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:56.230
and then sends that
to the backend.

00:21:56.230 --> 00:21:58.550
Flow Callout lets me
execute a sheared flow.

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:00.040
So let's say I've
got a flow that I

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:02.308
want to use in many of these
different APIs proxies.

00:22:02.308 --> 00:22:03.850
I could build a
shared flow for that.

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:05.500
And I access it
with a Flow Callout.

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:07.810
That lets me reuse code.

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:10.000
The Statistics
Collector will allow

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:13.067
me to store a variable
in the analytic system.

00:22:13.067 --> 00:22:14.650
So if I want to build
a custom report,

00:22:14.650 --> 00:22:17.020
I can grab data from
this custom variable.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:21.060
And I can show over a
custom report with that.

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:23.490
I can log something
to a logging system.

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:27.055
Maybe if this rate limit is hit
too often, I might log that.

00:22:27.055 --> 00:22:28.680
And then there's a
number of extensions

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:31.320
that we can use that make
our backend systems easier

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:32.628
to work with.

00:22:32.628 --> 00:22:34.920
So that's the basic policies
that are available to you.

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:41.610
Let's look at a few of
the policies I used.

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.110
First thing I'm going to do in
here is check for an API key.

00:22:44.110 --> 00:22:47.592
And I'm looking for an API
key as a query parameter.

00:22:47.592 --> 00:22:50.050
If I don't get one, this is
going to return an Unauthorized

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:51.010
error.

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:51.872
I'm rate limiting.

00:22:51.872 --> 00:22:54.080
I'm limiting somebody to 10
calls per minute in here.

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:56.710
If they call faster than
that, we'll return an error.

00:22:56.710 --> 00:22:58.120
I'm checking a quota.

00:22:58.120 --> 00:22:59.883
The quota is based
off of an API product.

00:22:59.883 --> 00:23:02.050
So we can have different
quotas for different people

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:03.830
calling this.

00:23:03.830 --> 00:23:05.330
We've got a response
cache in there.

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:06.997
So if I already have
the answer to this,

00:23:06.997 --> 00:23:09.112
if somebody sends me the
same request in a row,

00:23:09.112 --> 00:23:10.570
I'm going to grab
that from memory.

00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:11.950
And there's my
shared flow that's

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:15.050
going to log me
into Google Cloud.

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:19.240
So that's a few things I do
with every call that comes in.

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:21.310
This flow is where I
look up weather stations.

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:23.860
So someone specified
a particular location,

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:26.127
an airport, or a city,
or something like that.

00:23:26.127 --> 00:23:28.460
And I want to find weather
stations that are near there.

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:29.890
So the first thing
I do is, I'm going

00:23:29.890 --> 00:23:31.210
to make a call to Google Maps.

00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:32.585
But I need something
before that.

00:23:32.585 --> 00:23:35.320
I need my API key that
gets me into Google Maps.

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:37.555
So I'm looking that up
from the key value map.

00:23:37.555 --> 00:23:39.430
From there, I'm going
to construct a request.

00:23:39.430 --> 00:23:41.710
And I'm going to send
that address or location

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:42.550
into Google Maps.

00:23:42.550 --> 00:23:45.010
I'm going to ask it what's
the latitude and longitude.

00:23:45.010 --> 00:23:48.110
And I need to pass along
an API key to identify me.

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:49.952
And then I'll call
this URL down here.

00:23:49.952 --> 00:23:51.910
That's going to respond
back with a description

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:52.780
and the location.

00:23:52.780 --> 00:23:55.180
And that has a lot of
information in there.

00:23:55.180 --> 00:23:56.620
There's two pieces
of information

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:57.580
that I'm really interested in.

00:23:57.580 --> 00:23:59.288
Really the only thing
I want out of there

00:23:59.288 --> 00:24:02.660
is the latitude and longitude
of that particular location.

00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:04.720
So that is buried in
a certain section.

00:24:04.720 --> 00:24:06.730
That's under geometry.location.

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:08.810
And underneath there are
latitude and longitude.

00:24:08.810 --> 00:24:11.290
So I've got a JSON
path in here to show

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:12.610
where those variables are.

00:24:12.610 --> 00:24:17.260
And then I'm going to store the
values of those in lat and lon.

00:24:17.260 --> 00:24:19.780
Next, I'll construct
a bounding box

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:21.650
around that particular location.

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:24.850
So what this JavaScript
code is going to do

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:28.090
is create four variables down
there-- a minimum longitude,

00:24:28.090 --> 00:24:30.310
a minimum latitude,
a maximum longitude,

00:24:30.310 --> 00:24:31.870
and a maximum latitude.

00:24:31.870 --> 00:24:33.822
And those are
stored as variables

00:24:33.822 --> 00:24:35.530
that I can then access
later in the flow.

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:38.270
That's what all this code does.

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:40.330
And then next, I'll
construct the query in here.

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:42.790
So I've taken all
this information

00:24:42.790 --> 00:24:44.380
that I have gathered,
and I've used

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:46.482
that to build this query up.

00:24:46.482 --> 00:24:48.940
Now, when somebody called this,
they just sent to location.

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:50.350
And it didn't have
anything to do

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:52.683
with databases or any kind
of query language or anything

00:24:52.683 --> 00:24:53.540
like that.

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:55.180
So I'm taking the
request that was sent

00:24:55.180 --> 00:24:57.140
in that contained that address.

00:24:57.140 --> 00:24:59.710
I've gone and looked it up,
and calculated some latitudes

00:24:59.710 --> 00:25:01.120
and longitudes.

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:02.770
And I've built this query.

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:04.933
And that's what I'm going
to send to the backend.

00:25:04.933 --> 00:25:06.100
So that goes to the backend.

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:10.390
I've completely replaced the
payload in here with this.

00:25:10.390 --> 00:25:11.870
That goes to the backend.

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:13.120
And then we'll get a response.

00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:15.470
And then down here, I
reformat the response

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:18.440
so that it looks a
little bit nicer.

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:21.302
So I'm really just taking
the code that comes back

00:25:21.302 --> 00:25:23.260
and reformatting it to
make a little bit nicer.

00:25:23.260 --> 00:25:24.940
And I'm sorting it by distance.

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:28.747
So that's the basic flow.

00:25:28.747 --> 00:25:30.330
If we go in a little
more detail here,

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:33.000
we want to look up when
somebody has a weather station,

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:36.580
and they make a request,
what does that look like?

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:38.520
So they make a call in
with slash temperature.

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:40.740
And they're going to supply
a few things on the URL.

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:43.115
I need to grab that
data off of the URL.

00:25:43.115 --> 00:25:44.490
And then I'm going
to go and find

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:47.933
the data for that particular
weather station in that year.

00:25:47.933 --> 00:25:49.350
So this first
policy here is going

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:51.630
to grab the variables
they need from the URL.

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:53.610
So they're calling
slash stations.

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:56.280
They've supplied a weather
station ID right there,

00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:57.480
and it's followed by years.

00:25:57.480 --> 00:25:59.550
And then a particular
year is supplied there.

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:01.680
So I want to grab these
two pieces of information

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:04.290
and store them as variables.

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:07.230
And then my next step in here
is to build a query for this.

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:09.480
And I'm going to send
that into BigQuery.

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:12.973
And it's going to return
a big set of data for me.

00:26:12.973 --> 00:26:15.390
And again, I've replaced the
request with a different one.

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:19.660
This is a Statistics Collector.

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:22.300
And it's going to save the
weather station ID in case

00:26:22.300 --> 00:26:24.345
I want a report on
traffic by weather station

00:26:24.345 --> 00:26:26.220
ID to see which weather
stations are popular.

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:32.810
So that's how I
built my API proxy.

00:26:32.810 --> 00:26:34.580
This is a bit more
complicated than what

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:35.600
most people would build.

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:37.940
Most people are going to
take a backend service.

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:40.670
And they might just be
collecting a security token

00:26:40.670 --> 00:26:44.750
and then sending the response
back without any modification.

00:26:44.750 --> 00:26:46.395
And it's a great place to start.

00:26:46.395 --> 00:26:48.020
I wanted to show this
to you because it

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:49.640
shows all of the different
things you could do

00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:51.080
with Apigee if you wanted to.

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:55.710
Much simpler things can be
done, and that's a lot easier.

00:26:55.710 --> 00:26:59.390
So when I was building this,
it didn't quite work at first.

00:26:59.390 --> 00:27:01.290
I had the request
in the wrong format.

00:27:01.290 --> 00:27:03.620
And when I sent it to
BigQuery on the backend,

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:05.090
it returned an error.

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:07.580
And what I realized was,
something in my request

00:27:07.580 --> 00:27:09.110
was a little bit off.

00:27:09.110 --> 00:27:11.240
So I ran over to the
Trace tool over here.

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:13.640
And this helped me debug.

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:16.292
Let's start a trace session
and see how this works.

00:27:16.292 --> 00:27:18.500
So I'm going to click on
the button there to start up

00:27:18.500 --> 00:27:20.060
my trace session.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.490
And then I'm going to go
out to my client application

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:25.067
and send in a few requests.

00:27:25.067 --> 00:27:26.900
Let's go back to something
that's not cached

00:27:26.900 --> 00:27:27.858
and see how that looks.

00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:39.150
So we'll see requests start to
come streaming through in here.

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:44.130
I'll run a few of these so
we have a variety of results.

00:27:44.130 --> 00:27:46.040
So here we see a call entering.

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:48.170
And this one is for
temperature data

00:27:48.170 --> 00:27:50.540
for 2014 for this
particular weather station.

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:53.190
And there's our API
key on the end of that.

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:55.410
So we see this coming in.

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:57.125
We see the request content.

00:28:01.123 --> 00:28:03.290
Let's look at the steps and
see how it got that way.

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:05.560
So there's the API key check.

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:07.810
What we're seeing in
here is a whole bunch

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:09.640
of information about this.

00:28:09.640 --> 00:28:12.850
So when the API key is
evaluated by Apigee,

00:28:12.850 --> 00:28:14.243
it loads up a ton of metadata.

00:28:14.243 --> 00:28:15.160
And you can use these.

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:16.870
All these become
variables in here.

00:28:16.870 --> 00:28:19.837
And they can be used throughout
your flow for different things.

00:28:19.837 --> 00:28:21.670
Maybe you want to
dynamically route somebody

00:28:21.670 --> 00:28:24.790
to a different server based on
some sort of metadata that's

00:28:24.790 --> 00:28:27.660
associated with this key.

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:31.650
We'll see the spike arrest and
the quota policy being checked.

00:28:31.650 --> 00:28:33.420
It's looking to see
if we have the cache.

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:35.700
There's a False there to say
it wasn't found in cache.

00:28:35.700 --> 00:28:37.920
And then this is
my shared flow that

00:28:37.920 --> 00:28:40.010
authorizes me with BigQuery.

00:28:43.253 --> 00:28:44.920
We'll see the variables
being extracted.

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:46.570
And then the final
request is sent in.

00:28:46.570 --> 00:28:48.487
And we can see that this
looks very different.

00:28:48.487 --> 00:28:51.190
So we saw it came
in as a GET here.

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:52.880
And then it went out like this.

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:55.630
So we're seeing it
turned into a POST.

00:28:55.630 --> 00:28:57.830
So there's the POST.

00:28:57.830 --> 00:28:59.890
And that's what's going
out to the backend.

00:28:59.890 --> 00:29:01.870
We see the response coming back.

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:03.970
So we can see the format
of the data that's

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:05.620
returned from BigQuery.

00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:07.930
So we can see that I changed
that around a little bit,

00:29:07.930 --> 00:29:09.555
made it a little bit
cleaner and easier

00:29:09.555 --> 00:29:11.390
for the developer to understand.

00:29:11.390 --> 00:29:12.860
So that's a basic flow in here.

00:29:12.860 --> 00:29:15.050
The second one, we'll
notice, is a bit shorter.

00:29:15.050 --> 00:29:17.050
And that's because
we're hitting cache,

00:29:17.050 --> 00:29:18.677
so the response has
been returned to us

00:29:18.677 --> 00:29:19.510
straight from cache.

00:29:19.510 --> 00:29:20.593
And it's a lot faster too.

00:29:24.520 --> 00:29:25.600
Here's our rate limiting.

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:29.320
So in this case,
Apigee Edge detected

00:29:29.320 --> 00:29:32.350
that this is being called too
quickly, and it shut it down.

00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:35.620
So I've got this spike arrest
right here that was tripped.

00:29:35.620 --> 00:29:38.138
And then we'll see that a custom
error message is sent back.

00:29:38.138 --> 00:29:40.180
And it says, call rate
exceeded, this application

00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:41.722
is limited to 10
requests per minute.

00:29:41.722 --> 00:29:43.847
And that will give our
developer a pretty good hint

00:29:43.847 --> 00:29:44.810
about what's going on.

00:29:44.810 --> 00:29:48.260
So you have a good idea of
exactly what went wrong.

00:29:48.260 --> 00:29:50.565
Now, when I was
building this API proxy,

00:29:50.565 --> 00:29:52.690
I found out that I had
something a little bit wrong

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:53.570
in that query.

00:29:53.570 --> 00:29:55.880
So I had gotten a quote
in the wrong place,

00:29:55.880 --> 00:29:57.550
and so BigQuery
was rejecting it.

00:29:57.550 --> 00:29:59.020
And I saw that in
the Trace tool.

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:00.730
When I looked at the
payload, I could see

00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:01.720
that the mistake was in there.

00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:02.780
I went back and fixed it.

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:04.390
So this was really
helpful for me.

00:30:04.390 --> 00:30:07.270
It helped me find that
mistake in a big hurry,

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:09.620
and I was able to fix it.

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:12.640
So that's how we would
debug and trace something.

00:30:12.640 --> 00:30:14.950
The next thing to think
about is publishing.

00:30:14.950 --> 00:30:16.780
How do we take
this and publish it

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:19.700
out so that somebody can use it?

00:30:19.700 --> 00:30:22.222
So I want to talk about the
concept of API products.

00:30:22.222 --> 00:30:23.680
And this will become
important when

00:30:23.680 --> 00:30:25.960
we start to think about
how to publish our APIs out

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:28.010
for consumption.

00:30:28.010 --> 00:30:30.280
So we use API products
in Apigee Edge

00:30:30.280 --> 00:30:32.320
to share your APIs
with consumers.

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:34.360
And let's think about
this for a minute.

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:36.580
We're not just thinking
about services anymore.

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:38.230
These are now becoming products.

00:30:38.230 --> 00:30:41.080
And so there's a whole
lifecycle of these APIs

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:42.267
that people want to consume.

00:30:42.267 --> 00:30:44.350
We've got to think about
how we might manage that.

00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:45.830
So we're going to take a
formal approach to this,

00:30:45.830 --> 00:30:47.747
and we're going to think
of these as products.

00:30:47.747 --> 00:30:50.440
And a product might contain
more than one service.

00:30:50.440 --> 00:30:52.810
So what that looks like
inside of Apigee Edge

00:30:52.810 --> 00:30:55.460
is, we've got those APIs
proxies over there on the left.

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:57.460
And that's what you saw
me walking through here.

00:30:57.460 --> 00:30:59.410
I got this particular
Weather API.

00:30:59.410 --> 00:31:01.780
And I can have a
number of products

00:31:01.780 --> 00:31:02.890
that give access to that.

00:31:02.890 --> 00:31:06.700
So I have Silver access, Gold
access, and Platinum access

00:31:06.700 --> 00:31:07.570
as an example here.

00:31:07.570 --> 00:31:08.890
And they give you
different entitlements.

00:31:08.890 --> 00:31:10.515
And those are different
metadata that's

00:31:10.515 --> 00:31:12.650
associated with those products.

00:31:12.650 --> 00:31:14.470
So Silver gives us
100 calls per day

00:31:14.470 --> 00:31:16.690
with 5 calls per minute
and some limited access.

00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:18.110
We can only do some things.

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:20.110
So, for example, I might
only be able to look up

00:31:20.110 --> 00:31:23.050
weather stations, but I can't
get temperature or wind data.

00:31:23.050 --> 00:31:25.597
And Gold access might
entitle me to more calls.

00:31:25.597 --> 00:31:28.180
And it would give me full access
to all those different things

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:30.280
that are in the APIs.

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:32.470
So I have a series
of these products,

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:33.880
but people don't
have access yet.

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:36.820
If I want to grant somebody
access to call this API,

00:31:36.820 --> 00:31:38.510
I'm going to register
an application.

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:42.280
So let's say we're building the
Android application for looking

00:31:42.280 --> 00:31:43.630
at weather history.

00:31:43.630 --> 00:31:46.870
I'd register my application
and some metadata associated

00:31:46.870 --> 00:31:47.380
with that.

00:31:47.380 --> 00:31:49.330
And that would generate
some keys for me

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:51.050
that I could use to
call into the system.

00:31:51.050 --> 00:31:54.040
So you register an
application, and that gives you

00:31:54.040 --> 00:31:55.600
access to one or more products.

00:31:55.600 --> 00:31:57.160
And those products
have entitlements

00:31:57.160 --> 00:31:58.840
to use certain APIs.

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:01.603
So we don't just have a
service, and everybody calls it,

00:32:01.603 --> 00:32:02.770
and we don't know who's who.

00:32:02.770 --> 00:32:05.740
We're going to have a system
in here for managing products.

00:32:05.740 --> 00:32:07.960
And we give people
access to these products.

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:09.580
They have API keys,
or they're going

00:32:09.580 --> 00:32:11.950
to be sending OAuth
tokens or JWTs.

00:32:11.950 --> 00:32:15.220
And that lets us track
exactly who's calling things.

00:32:15.220 --> 00:32:17.350
So where do we get these keys?

00:32:17.350 --> 00:32:19.473
We create an API product
inside of Apigee,

00:32:19.473 --> 00:32:20.890
where you decide
which proxies are

00:32:20.890 --> 00:32:23.950
going to go in there and
which resources of those.

00:32:23.950 --> 00:32:26.680
From there, we register
a developer application.

00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:30.640
And a developer can have access
to more than one API product.

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:32.980
And that generates two
keys-- a consumer key

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:34.420
and a consumer secret key.

00:32:34.420 --> 00:32:36.620
And you use the consumer
key as your API key.

00:32:36.620 --> 00:32:38.620
Or you're going to use
both keys if you're going

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:40.660
to generate an OAuth token.

00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:42.730
So that's a quick
overview of API products.

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:45.670
Let's go back and see how
we do that inside of Apigee.

00:32:45.670 --> 00:32:48.112
So let's click over
here on the box.

00:32:48.112 --> 00:32:49.570
And we'll see that
in this section,

00:32:49.570 --> 00:32:52.410
we've got something
called API Products.

00:32:52.410 --> 00:32:54.160
And I'll show you an
API product that I've

00:32:54.160 --> 00:32:57.787
built for my Weather demo.

00:32:57.787 --> 00:32:59.120
So we have the Weather History--

00:32:59.120 --> 00:33:00.313
Try it out down here.

00:33:00.313 --> 00:33:02.480
And let me put this in edit
mode so you can see what

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:04.490
it was like when I created it.

00:33:04.490 --> 00:33:06.110
So I called this
Weather History--

00:33:06.110 --> 00:33:07.310
Try it.

00:33:07.310 --> 00:33:10.670
And it provides 100 calls per
day for development usage.

00:33:10.670 --> 00:33:12.290
And the access is
public, meaning

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:13.820
it'll show up on my portal.

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:17.200
I've got 100 requests for every
one day, and that's the quota.

00:33:20.270 --> 00:33:23.090
This describes what's
available in my API.

00:33:23.090 --> 00:33:24.890
So anybody who
calls that you URL,

00:33:24.890 --> 00:33:27.440
I could specify in here
something a little more

00:33:27.440 --> 00:33:30.920
detailed, like slash
temperature or slash stations,

00:33:30.920 --> 00:33:32.460
if I wanted to.

00:33:32.460 --> 00:33:35.210
And then we can see down here
all the different developer

00:33:35.210 --> 00:33:37.150
apps that make use
of this product.

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:43.240
Let's look at the
webcast-demo-app.

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:50.960
So here's our application.

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:54.410
We can see that it's
usage was approved.

00:33:54.410 --> 00:33:56.120
And we have a key
and a secret that we

00:33:56.120 --> 00:33:57.350
can use for calling this.

00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:02.470
If I wanted to add
custom attributes,

00:34:02.470 --> 00:34:03.910
I can do so down here.

00:34:03.910 --> 00:34:06.910
I might have partner ID on there
or something like that that's

00:34:06.910 --> 00:34:08.110
meaningful to my business.

00:34:08.110 --> 00:34:10.315
And whenever this API
key comes through here,

00:34:10.315 --> 00:34:11.690
that variable
would be loaded up.

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:15.130
And I can use it for logging,
or for traffic redirection,

00:34:15.130 --> 00:34:17.469
or figuring out
how to limit them,

00:34:17.469 --> 00:34:20.179
or whatever I want
to do with it.

00:34:20.179 --> 00:34:22.850
So this is one way to create
an application and register it.

00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:24.100
You can do it by hand in here.

00:34:24.100 --> 00:34:26.580
And I can register this
for a particular developer.

00:34:26.580 --> 00:34:28.330
I send these credentials
to the developer,

00:34:28.330 --> 00:34:29.780
and they're good to go.

00:34:29.780 --> 00:34:32.110
But another way to do that,
probably a more common way

00:34:32.110 --> 00:34:34.630
to do that, is that the
developer would do this.

00:34:34.630 --> 00:34:37.659
And they're going to do that
using a Developer Portal.

00:34:37.659 --> 00:34:41.110
So our third perspective
is the API team.

00:34:41.110 --> 00:34:43.179
Let's look at how
they would use Apigee

00:34:43.179 --> 00:34:45.219
to manage their API program.

00:34:45.219 --> 00:34:48.500
The API team has an interest in
knowing how things are running.

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:51.070
So they may come in to
the Analyze tab over here

00:34:51.070 --> 00:34:53.620
and look at some
