00:37:49.635 --> 00:37:51.480
running smoothly.

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:52.770
So let's take some questions.

00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:55.190
So first question
in here is, how

00:37:55.190 --> 00:37:58.220
do you do versioning in Apigee?

00:37:58.220 --> 00:37:59.762
So good question.

00:37:59.762 --> 00:38:00.470
I get this a lot.

00:38:00.470 --> 00:38:04.520
So there's quite a bit of
religion around versioning.

00:38:04.520 --> 00:38:06.290
What I typically
recommend to people

00:38:06.290 --> 00:38:08.630
is that you've got a URL.

00:38:08.630 --> 00:38:10.970
And that URL is going to have
the version number in it.

00:38:10.970 --> 00:38:14.870
So put a v1 or a v2 in
your URL as far to the left

00:38:14.870 --> 00:38:16.400
as you possibly can.

00:38:16.400 --> 00:38:20.960
So what you do in Apigee
is, you can take your proxy,

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:22.573
and you can save it
as a new revision.

00:38:22.573 --> 00:38:23.990
And then inside
of there, you just

00:38:23.990 --> 00:38:25.610
change the URL that
it's listening on.

00:38:25.610 --> 00:38:28.340
So you might have one
v1-weather-demo, for example,

00:38:28.340 --> 00:38:29.840
or v2-weather-demo.

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:32.310
And then you're free to
change that around as needed.

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:33.830
So that's versioning.

00:38:33.830 --> 00:38:36.230
There's also the
concept of revisioning.

00:38:36.230 --> 00:38:38.623
So Apigee has a built
in revisioning system.

00:38:38.623 --> 00:38:40.040
You might have
noticed in the demo

00:38:40.040 --> 00:38:42.890
that I was on revision
7 and 8 in there.

00:38:42.890 --> 00:38:44.570
The way that works
is, that's going

00:38:44.570 --> 00:38:47.540
to allow you to roll back to
previous versions of your API

00:38:47.540 --> 00:38:48.260
proxy.

00:38:48.260 --> 00:38:50.450
And in some cases, you
might make changes in there

00:38:50.450 --> 00:38:52.970
that I would consider a
revision but not a version.

00:38:52.970 --> 00:38:55.460
So I would consider a
version something that breaks

00:38:55.460 --> 00:38:57.320
the contract with the client.

00:38:57.320 --> 00:38:59.228
And if you change around
the payload that's

00:38:59.228 --> 00:39:01.520
sent back in such a way that
it would break the client,

00:39:01.520 --> 00:39:03.890
then that needs to be a
new version with a new URL.

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:06.140
If you're just adding
caching or fixing the way

00:39:06.140 --> 00:39:08.450
that your proxy works, but
that doesn't necessarily

00:39:08.450 --> 00:39:10.430
change the data that
goes back and forth,

00:39:10.430 --> 00:39:11.840
that's just a revision.

00:39:11.840 --> 00:39:13.940
So those two things together
are going to make up

00:39:13.940 --> 00:39:17.400
revisioning and versioning.

00:39:17.400 --> 00:39:20.370
Will Apigee export a
proxy to OpenAPI format

00:39:20.370 --> 00:39:21.720
as well as import it that way?

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:24.780
You can definitely
import one that way.

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:26.670
There's a whole spec
editor inside of Apigee.

00:39:26.670 --> 00:39:28.800
And you can edit
your specs in there,

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:31.720
and you can publish them
out to the portal that way.

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:33.480
We do have some
tools out on GitHub

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:36.150
that will introspect
an existing API proxy

00:39:36.150 --> 00:39:39.510
and make an attempt to
generate an OpenAPI spec.

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:43.122
If you work with a WSDL, and
you're doing a SOAP service,

00:39:43.122 --> 00:39:45.330
that part of the SOAP wizard
there will automatically

00:39:45.330 --> 00:39:48.198
generate one for you.

00:39:48.198 --> 00:39:49.740
What about role-based
access control?

00:39:49.740 --> 00:39:51.115
How do we keep
people from seeing

00:39:51.115 --> 00:39:52.320
stuff they shouldn't see?

00:39:52.320 --> 00:39:52.940
Good question.

00:39:52.940 --> 00:39:53.940
As I went through this--

00:39:53.940 --> 00:39:54.720
I'm the administrator.

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:56.470
And I can see all kinds
of stuff in there,

00:39:56.470 --> 00:39:57.720
and I can push to production.

00:39:57.720 --> 00:40:00.630
A typical way of doing this
is, there's a role-based access

00:40:00.630 --> 00:40:01.920
system in there.

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:04.570
And we would use that to control
what people have access to.

00:40:04.570 --> 00:40:08.520
So I might have access to
a staging place or a QA

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:11.190
environment, but I don't
have access to production.

00:40:11.190 --> 00:40:13.440
And we might use
service APIs in order

00:40:13.440 --> 00:40:14.770
to push things into production.

00:40:14.770 --> 00:40:18.030
So we might use the UI for
some of our development work.

00:40:18.030 --> 00:40:20.370
But then we're going to check
the source code into Git.

00:40:20.370 --> 00:40:21.640
We're going to pull
down from there,

00:40:21.640 --> 00:40:22.680
maybe run some tests on it.

00:40:22.680 --> 00:40:24.555
And then we'll have that
automatically pushed

00:40:24.555 --> 00:40:26.970
into production.

00:40:26.970 --> 00:40:28.740
Is it possible to
combine the results

00:40:28.740 --> 00:40:30.420
from more than one
backend building

00:40:30.420 --> 00:40:32.130
aggregate or a composite?

00:40:32.130 --> 00:40:32.700
It is.

00:40:32.700 --> 00:40:36.695
So go out on YouTube and
search for "Apigee Up Close."

00:40:36.695 --> 00:40:39.070
And you'll find that I did a
whole webcast on that topic.

00:40:39.070 --> 00:40:40.920
So just in summary,
what you do is,

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:43.003
you can call out to two
or three backend services.

00:40:43.003 --> 00:40:45.337
And then typically, you would
use one of the programming

00:40:45.337 --> 00:40:45.840
languages--

00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:47.670
Java, JavaScript, or Python--

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:49.830
and build a composite result
in there to send back.

00:40:49.830 --> 00:40:52.950
But yeah, we've got a
whole webcast on that.

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:56.070
Is Apigee only for people that
have external API programs?

00:40:56.070 --> 00:40:58.770
Do people use it for
internal API programs?

00:40:58.770 --> 00:40:59.280
They do.

00:40:59.280 --> 00:41:01.050
So yeah, oddly
enough, most people

00:41:01.050 --> 00:41:02.700
are using it for
internal API programs.

00:41:02.700 --> 00:41:06.390
We have a small subset of people
who have external-facing APIs.

00:41:06.390 --> 00:41:08.255
And it's fantastic for that.

00:41:08.255 --> 00:41:09.630
But the same kind
of benefits you

00:41:09.630 --> 00:41:12.210
might get when you're running
an external API program,

00:41:12.210 --> 00:41:13.950
those could be used
inside your company

00:41:13.950 --> 00:41:16.080
for people that are
external to your group.

00:41:16.080 --> 00:41:20.340
So same concept but
used internally.

00:41:20.340 --> 00:41:23.340
Does Apigee support
OpenAPI spec version 3?

00:41:23.340 --> 00:41:24.750
Yes, it does.

00:41:24.750 --> 00:41:27.720
So yeah, you can try that
out in the spec editor.

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:30.000
Can Apigee return a
different response

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:33.165
based on an Accept header,
for example, JSON or XML?

00:41:33.165 --> 00:41:35.040
Yes, that's something
we see pretty commonly.

00:41:35.040 --> 00:41:37.200
So let's say you have an
existing SOAP service,

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:40.590
and you want to have that
return either XML or JSON

00:41:40.590 --> 00:41:42.570
depending on how
somebody calls it.

00:41:42.570 --> 00:41:44.280
So you can put a
condition in there.

00:41:44.280 --> 00:41:47.130
And that condition would be
used to determine whether it

00:41:47.130 --> 00:41:48.900
runs the XML-to-JSON policy.

00:41:48.900 --> 00:41:51.150
And that would return it--
it would behave differently

00:41:51.150 --> 00:41:53.442
depending on how you call
it, which header you send in.

00:41:53.442 --> 00:41:56.980
So nice way and very easy
to build an intelligent API

00:41:56.980 --> 00:41:57.480
like that.

00:41:57.480 --> 00:42:00.270
It's going to respond
with what people need.

00:42:00.270 --> 00:42:03.720
Can you use Apigee to control
access to a cloud function?

00:42:03.720 --> 00:42:05.970
You certainly can, so it's
a common use case for that.

00:42:05.970 --> 00:42:07.740
You might have a
service account enabled

00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:09.222
for your cloud function.

00:42:09.222 --> 00:42:10.930
And then you'd put
Apigee in front of it,

00:42:10.930 --> 00:42:13.560
and only Apigee would be able
to access that with credentials.

00:42:13.560 --> 00:42:16.230
And then you might manage the
security to the outside world

00:42:16.230 --> 00:42:19.770
with OAuth tokens that
are managed by Apigee.

00:42:19.770 --> 00:42:22.410
Can you export Analytics
data using an API?

00:42:22.410 --> 00:42:24.120
Yes, there's a
whole API in there

00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:25.380
for getting an Analytics data.

00:42:25.380 --> 00:42:27.990
Check the Apigee documentation,
and within there, you'll

00:42:27.990 --> 00:42:29.520
see that there's an API.

00:42:29.520 --> 00:42:32.220
You can specify a time range,
and you can pull down Analytics

00:42:32.220 --> 00:42:35.220
data programmatically.

00:42:35.220 --> 00:42:36.540
Let's say the backend changes.

00:42:36.540 --> 00:42:39.480
Can you pre-populate or
invalidate that cache

00:42:39.480 --> 00:42:40.780
that you're using in there?

00:42:40.780 --> 00:42:41.610
So yes, you can.

00:42:41.610 --> 00:42:44.010
There's policies to adjust
the cache programmatically.

00:42:44.010 --> 00:42:46.350
You could also run
that through the UI.

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:48.750
There's a tool in there
to flush the cache.

00:42:48.750 --> 00:42:51.330
And you can do it through
a service API, where

00:42:51.330 --> 00:42:52.595
you call the cache directly.

00:42:52.595 --> 00:42:54.720
And you could pre-populate
it or you could flush it

00:42:54.720 --> 00:42:57.670
if the backend is changed.

00:42:57.670 --> 00:43:00.670
Can you route traffic to
different backend servers

00:43:00.670 --> 00:43:02.680
based on something you
know about the identity

00:43:02.680 --> 00:43:04.010
of the calling application?

00:43:04.010 --> 00:43:06.160
So, for example,
partner A and partner B

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:08.710
both call the same URL,
but they should be routed

00:43:08.710 --> 00:43:10.570
to different backend servers.

00:43:10.570 --> 00:43:11.080
Sure.

00:43:11.080 --> 00:43:13.300
So what is typically
done in there

00:43:13.300 --> 00:43:17.020
is, when you generate an
API key or an OAuth token,

00:43:17.020 --> 00:43:18.880
you can apply custom
metadata to that.

00:43:18.880 --> 00:43:20.650
So you would put in
there something that

00:43:20.650 --> 00:43:22.480
lets you know which
partner this is

00:43:22.480 --> 00:43:26.860
or gives an idea of some sort
of identity that you can use.

00:43:26.860 --> 00:43:29.960
That becomes a variable
when their call comes in.

00:43:29.960 --> 00:43:32.860
So as soon as that processes
the OAuth token or the API key,

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:34.653
that data is loaded
as a variable.

00:43:34.653 --> 00:43:36.070
And you have a
condition in there.

00:43:36.070 --> 00:43:38.530
And you can supply multiple
backend targets inside

00:43:38.530 --> 00:43:40.550
of Apigee, and you can
set up a route rule.

00:43:40.550 --> 00:43:42.040
So that's all built
in to do that.

00:43:42.040 --> 00:43:44.415
You would look at that attribute
and then route to A or B

00:43:44.415 --> 00:43:46.440
based on that.

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:49.590
Is it possible to have different
quotas for the same API?

00:43:49.590 --> 00:43:51.690
So, for example,
person A and person B

00:43:51.690 --> 00:43:54.870
both called the same URL but
are limited in different ways.

00:43:54.870 --> 00:43:55.470
It is.

00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:58.980
So oftentimes, we'd put a
quota on the API product.

00:43:58.980 --> 00:44:00.900
And then, when you
go to generate a key,

00:44:00.900 --> 00:44:02.980
you would either choose
one product or the other,

00:44:02.980 --> 00:44:04.813
depending on what you
want that quota to be.

00:44:04.813 --> 00:44:08.940
So when those two people call
that API using different API

00:44:08.940 --> 00:44:12.440
keys, they'd be rate
limited differently.

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:14.690
What are the key features
of an API Gateway?

00:44:14.690 --> 00:44:17.330
So I made a point in
here of distinguishing

00:44:17.330 --> 00:44:20.690
between a Gateway and an
API management platform.

00:44:20.690 --> 00:44:22.795
A Gateway tends to be
more security focused.

00:44:22.795 --> 00:44:24.170
So this is the
gateway that we're

00:44:24.170 --> 00:44:25.550
going to use to let people in.

00:44:25.550 --> 00:44:28.670
And it's going to check OAuth
tokens and send them along

00:44:28.670 --> 00:44:30.930
if they have one and
block them if they don't.

00:44:30.930 --> 00:44:34.460
So a Gateway tends to be more
around that, more networking

00:44:34.460 --> 00:44:35.030
based.

00:44:35.030 --> 00:44:36.710
And then an API
management platform

00:44:36.710 --> 00:44:38.520
is considering all these people.

00:44:38.520 --> 00:44:41.720
So it expands on the
Gateway with the ability

00:44:41.720 --> 00:44:44.120
to manage developers,
to manage keys,

00:44:44.120 --> 00:44:46.020
to have Analytics
and things like that.

00:44:46.020 --> 00:44:48.977
So that's a much bigger
solution than a simple gateway.

00:44:48.977 --> 00:44:50.810
Sometimes, a Gateway
is appropriate for what

00:44:50.810 --> 00:44:53.227
you're doing, and sometimes
you need a full API management

00:44:53.227 --> 00:44:54.750
solution.

00:44:54.750 --> 00:44:57.438
So question in here about what
is the use of custom attributes

00:44:57.438 --> 00:44:57.980
and products.

00:44:57.980 --> 00:44:59.993
Can you share an example
where that's useful?

00:44:59.993 --> 00:45:01.910
So yeah, that goes back
to the other question.

00:45:01.910 --> 00:45:05.178
When I created an API product,
I might put a key on there.

00:45:05.178 --> 00:45:07.220
And that's going to be
propagated to all the apps

00:45:07.220 --> 00:45:08.130
that are generated.

00:45:08.130 --> 00:45:11.690
So basically, that
attribute will go on

00:45:11.690 --> 00:45:14.090
to any keys that are
generated for apps.

00:45:14.090 --> 00:45:16.490
And then it comes
through as a variable.

00:45:16.490 --> 00:45:19.350
And you might use that for
conditional expressions.

00:45:19.350 --> 00:45:21.898
So you might have a policy
that either executes

00:45:21.898 --> 00:45:22.940
or doesn't based on that.

00:45:22.940 --> 00:45:24.950
Or you may have routing rules
based on that to send them

00:45:24.950 --> 00:45:25.790
to different places.

00:45:25.790 --> 00:45:28.400
Or the quota might be
enforced based on that.

00:45:28.400 --> 00:45:30.810
I think that's about all
we've got for time today.

00:45:30.810 --> 00:45:32.670
So we can go ahead
and wrap it up.

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:34.320
Thank you so much
for attending today.

00:45:34.320 --> 00:45:35.360
We appreciate it.

00:45:35.360 --> 00:45:37.450
We'll see you in
the next webcast.

